## 1. The seven integration models

### [CXC-IM-CATALOG] Seven shapes for integrating with ComposeIO/ComposeBus

- claim: every service or application that joins the platform takes one of exactly seven shapes; each child node below states the shape's defining property and the node where that property was settled. Six are the capability/service shapes, unchanged since 2026-09-12; the seventh — declarative state_manager config — is an APPLICATION-only addition (2026-09-12), never consulted by the capability decision order in [CXC-IM-SERVICE-RUBRIC].
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the catalog's enumeration and grouping; each child's underlying claim is grounded individually.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTEGRATION-MODELS]
- see-also: [CXC-IM-MODEL-CS-PLUGIN], [CXC-IM-MODEL-STANDALONE], [CXC-IM-MODEL-INPROCESS], [CXC-IM-MODEL-BUS-PEER], [CXC-IM-MODEL-BRIDGE], [CXC-IM-MODEL-KERNEL], [CXC-IM-MODEL-STATE-MANAGER-CONFIG]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-CS-PLUGIN] ComposeServer plugin — per-tenant, hosted in one ComposeServer

- defining-property: per-tenant, hosted inside one ComposeServer; a single typed pydantic contract DERIVES the plugin's config form, announce payload, and registry entry — the author hand-writes none of the three.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — naming this as a catalog entry; the underlying contract and its per-tenant invariant are david-ruling, per the grounded nodes.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-PLUGIN-CONTRACT], [CXC-CS-MULTI-TENANCY]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-STANDALONE] Standalone process — bare-Python daemon / separate bus peer

- defining-property: a bare-Python daemon running as its own OS process, a separate bus peer rather than a plugin hosted inside a ComposeServer.
- superseded-for-fleet-services: this is the shape the ComposeServer plugin model SUPERSEDES for fleet services — bus_scribe was specified as a standalone fleet process and was later superseded into a default per-tenant plugin; see the grounded node for the worked case.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — naming this as a catalog entry; the supersession itself is david-ruling, per the grounded node.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [CXC-BUS-SCRIBE-DEPLOYMENT]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-INPROCESS] In-process source/thread inside an app

- defining-property: a data source or thread that lives inside an app's own process, pinned to that app's own session, rather than a separate OS process or bus peer.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — naming this as a catalog entry; the underlying edge-question and the worked decision are grounded in the cited nodes.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [DX-SESSION-OWNED-VS-DISTRIBUTED], [DEC-INPROCESS-SOURCES]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-BUS-PEER] Bus-native peer / leaf / device

- defining-property: rides ComposeBus as a co-equal peer, often running on constrained silicon; owns its own behavior and publishes semantic intent upward rather than exposing its mechanism.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — naming this as a catalog entry; the leaf-owns-behavior principle is grounded in the cited node.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [DX-LEAF-OWNS-BEHAVIOR]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-BRIDGE] Bridge — translates two live transports

- defining-property: translates between two live transports at the wire; complementary to the other five shapes, never an alternative to them — a bridge does not compete for where a capability's LOGIC lives, only for how two already-chosen endpoints are connected.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — naming this as a catalog entry; the collapse-the-transport-not-the-seam principle is grounded in the cited node.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [DX-COLLAPSE-TRANSPORT]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-KERNEL] Kernel built-in — only the irreducible residue

- defining-property: lives inside the ComposeServer micro-kernel itself (supervise, load, config-persist, bus-provision) rather than as a plugin; default is NO — almost nothing qualifies, because any capability that CAN be a plugin MUST be one, and the kernel is only what remains after that test.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — naming this as a catalog entry; the microkernel residue test and the default-plugin pattern are grounded in the cited nodes.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [CXC-CS-MICROKERNEL], [CXC-CS-DEFAULT-PLUGINS]

### [CXC-IM-MODEL-STATE-MANAGER-CONFIG] Declarative state_manager config — no dedicated process, no code

- defining-property: realize an application DECLARATIVELY as state_manager config/rules (a `state_profile`) — no dedicated process, no code. The application's behavior lives entirely in config consumed by the existing state_manager; there is nothing to author in a new language runtime and nothing to host as a separate peer.
- application-only: this shape is an APPLICATION path, not a capability shape — it is never a rung in [CXC-IM-SERVICE-RUBRIC]'s capability decision order; it is sited instead by [CXC-IM-Q0-APP-VS-CAPABILITY]'s application-path and [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS]'s spectrum.
- first-class-not-fallback: this is a FIRST-CLASS application path, not a fallback from the standalone or plugin shapes. Modeling a house in state_manager was one of the DIRECT use-case inspirations for state_manager in the first place (David, 2026-09-12) — the intended home for domains that fit a declarative model: home automation (a Home-Assistant replacement), simple games, and dashboards.
- grounded-in: [DX-DERIVE-ONCE-REALIZE-PER-LEAF] — the application IS a declarative state model, a fact derived once by the tier-floor evaluator and realized independently per leaf — and [DX-STATE-ON-BUS] — semantic state published once on the bus, each leaf/widget owning its own presentation. These are the same primitives state_manager already instantiates platform-wide; here they carry the WHOLE application rather than one capability's derivation.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-stated — the founding use-case (modeling a house in state_manager) and this shape's first-class status are David's own words, 2026-09-12: "I can imagine a simple ComposeIO based game or replacement-for-Home-Assistant use case where the existing tool set is more than sufficient. Modeling a house in state-manager was, in fact, one of the direct use-case inspirations for state-manager in the first place." The grounding in [DX-DERIVE-ONCE-REALIZE-PER-LEAF] / [DX-STATE-ON-BUS] is architect-synthesis.
- part-of: [CXC-IM-CATALOG]
- see-also: [DX-DERIVE-ONCE-REALIZE-PER-LEAF], [DX-STATE-ON-BUS], [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS], [CXC-IM-EXEMPLARS]

