## 2. The top fork — application or capability

### [CXC-IM-Q0-APP-VS-CAPABILITY] Q0 — is this an end-user APPLICATION, or a platform CAPABILITY/SERVICE?

- question: is this an end-user APPLICATION/PROJECT (a game, a domain world-model, a creative artifact under active iteration) or a platform CAPABILITY/SERVICE?
- application-path: an APPLICATION is placed by AUTHORSHIP-AND-ITERATION ergonomics, not by the service rubric below — but there is no single default shape. An APPLICATION's home sits on a USE-CASE x COMPLEXITY spectrum — declarative state-manager config <-> standalone app <-> plugin — and the end-user CHOOSES along it by the specifics of their own skill, their use of agentic AI, and the project's complexity. Deliberately NO hard thresholds or rule-sets decide this for them: the guidance is descriptive axes, not a prescriptive cutoff — see [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE]. See [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS] for the spectrum in detail.
- capability-path: a CAPABILITY/SERVICE is placed by the fixed decision order in [CXC-IM-SERVICE-RUBRIC].
- origin-of-fork: Prompted by David 2026-09-12: he raised the BSG world as an end-user project that "might be best implemented as a standalone process" and asked for a two-scenario steelman, leaving plugin / state-manager-config / standalone ALL explicitly open ("Maybe? Maybe? Maybe."). The determination that the BSG world IS an application (not a service) — and therefore that a Q0 application-vs-capability fork must precede the service rubric — is ARCHITECT-SYNTHESIS reached after reading the world code, NOT David's own assertion; he posed it as an open question and the architect resolved it. David then ratified the resulting SHAPE ("I think the revised shape holds"), which is what provenance:david-ratified-shape attests on this node — it does NOT attest that David himself classified BSG as application-not-service. (Corrected 2026-09-12 per the end-design-session audit finding C1, which caught the prior wording promoting the architect's determination to "David's correction".)
- application-path-reshape-2026-09-12: the `application-path` property above replaces an earlier "Default: a standalone bus-peer app" wording that over-generalized from the BSG steelman to every application. David's same-day follow-on clarification corrected this: "I think the guidance needs to be based on both use-case and complexity - and without exact rule sets so that the end-user can choose the right model based on the specifics of their skill, use of agentic AI, and project complexity." That correction is DAVID-STATED, carried here near-verbatim; the specific prose of `application-path` remains architect-synthesis.
- status: decided
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTEGRATION-MODELS]
- see-also: [CXC-IM-SERVICE-RUBRIC], [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS], [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE]

### [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE] The application guidance is deliberately DESCRIPTIVE axes, not a prescriptive rule-set

- stance: the guidance for siting an APPLICATION ([CXC-IM-Q0-APP-VS-CAPABILITY]'s application-path, [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS]'s spectrum) is DELIBERATELY DESCRIPTIVE — four axes the end-user weighs for their own specifics (use-case, complexity, author skill, agentic-AI use) — and NOT prescriptive: no numeric thresholds, no rule-set that would decide for them.
- why-no-thresholds: a numeric cutoff (lines of config, node count, tenant count) would substitute the rubric's judgment for the end-user's own — precisely the over-generalization David's clarification corrects. The axes orient the choice; they do not make it.
- status: decided
- provenance: david-stated — this IS David's own directed framing, 2026-09-12, quoted near-verbatim above.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTEGRATION-MODELS]
- see-also: [CXC-IM-Q0-APP-VS-CAPABILITY], [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS], [CXC-IM-MODEL-STATE-MANAGER-CONFIG]

