## 3. The service rubric — decision order for a CAPABILITY

### [CXC-IM-SERVICE-RUBRIC] The decision order for siting a CAPABILITY among the six models

- decision-order: a fixed, ordered test run top to bottom; the first matching rung decides, and nothing below it is consulted.
- order-1-bus-peer: constrained silicon, a physical device, or a cross-tier leaf — it cannot be a Python plugin at all, so it is a bus-native peer.
- order-2-bridge: the capability purely translates between two already-live transports — it is a bridge, not a competing home for the logic.
- order-3-kernel: the capability is an irreducible kernel primitive — default is NO; almost nothing qualifies, because anything that CAN be a plugin MUST be one.
- order-4-inprocess: the only consumer is ONE app, the capability is tied to that app's session, and it need not outlive it — it is an in-process source.
- order-5-plugin: none of the above — the DEFAULT is a ComposeServer plugin.
- fallback-to-standalone: fall back to a standalone process ONLY when a plugin cannot satisfy one of three conditions the plugin model structurally cannot meet — it must span MULTIPLE tenants/servers (a plugin is always per-tenant, never shared), it must run on a DIFFERENT host than any ComposeServer, or it must OUTLIVE the server. The standalone shape is an escape hatch FROM rung 5, not a sixth rung in the ordered test.
- then-tune-the-plugin: once rung 5 applies, the plugin is tuned by declaring an isolation FLOOR appropriate to its fault domain and a `requires:` execution floor naming what it needs to run.
- status: proposed
- provenance: architect-synthesis — the specific decision-order wording, numbering, and the fallback-to-standalone framing are the architect's inference from the underlying principles; the overall rubric SHAPE this order instantiates is david-ratified-shape, per [CXC-IM-Q0-APP-VS-CAPABILITY].
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTEGRATION-MODELS]
- see-also: [CXC-IM-MODEL-BUS-PEER], [CXC-IM-MODEL-BRIDGE], [CXC-IM-MODEL-KERNEL], [CXC-IM-MODEL-INPROCESS], [CXC-IM-MODEL-CS-PLUGIN], [CXC-IM-MODEL-STANDALONE], [CXC-CS-ISOLATION-FLOOR], [CXC-CS-REQUIRES-FLOOR]

---

## 4. The application authorship axis

### [CXC-IM-APP-AUTHORSHIP-AXIS] Where an APPLICATION sits on the use-case x complexity spectrum

- axis: for an APPLICATION (the Q0 application-path), its home sits on a USE-CASE x COMPLEXITY spectrum — declarative state-manager config <-> standalone app <-> plugin — rather than one default shape. The HAND-AUTHORED-vs-AGENTIC axis then shifts where a given application sits on that spectrum.
- declarative-config-end: state_manager config fits domains EXPRESSIBLE declaratively — home automation (a Home-Assistant replacement), simple games, dashboards ([CXC-IM-MODEL-STATE-MANAGER-CONFIG]). This is state_manager's FOUNDING use-case (David, 2026-09-12: modeling a house in state_manager was one of the direct use-case inspirations for state_manager itself) and a FIRST-CLASS end of the spectrum, not a fallback.
- standalone-code-end: standalone app is the home when the logic EXCEEDS natural declarative expression — a complex structural simulation, the BSG world being the worked case ([CXC-IM-EXEMPLARS]).
- plugin-end: see `promote-to-plugin-when` below — the plugin end is reached when the application is DONE being iterated and ships as a managed tenant, not an iteration-time default.
- hand-authored-rule: when a human hand-authors the logic, keep complex rules in CODE (pulling toward the standalone end) — a declarative DSL expressive enough to carry a structural simulation is a programming language in disguise, and usually a worse one than the host language.
- ai-assisted-rule: when authorship is AI-assisted, more of the AUTHORABLE rules (scenarios, tunables, escalations) can profitably move to a declarative, queryable, verifiable surface, because structure is the agent's leverage and its own verification surface.
- agentic-frontier-shifts-both-ways: AI assistance moves the frontier in BOTH directions at once — an AI can maintain a larger/more-complex declarative model than a human would find tedious, WIDENING where config stays viable toward more complex domains, AND it can write and iterate a standalone app fluently, LOWERING the code path's own cost. With agentic help the author's own coding skill matters less either way, and the choice turns more on which SHAPE — declarative or procedural — naturally fits the domain, per [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE]'s no-hard-thresholds stance.
- algorithmic-core-stays-code: the ALGORITHMIC CORE stays code regardless of authorship mode or which end of the spectrum an application otherwise sits on — the axis governs the AUTHORABLE rule surface, not the engine underneath it.
- promote-to-plugin-when: promote an application to a ComposeServer plugin only when "ship it as a managed, multi-tenant tenant" outweighs iteration freedom — i.e. when the application is DONE being iterated, not while it is still being built.
- no-hard-thresholds: as with [CXC-IM-Q0-APP-VS-CAPABILITY]'s application-path, deliberately NO numeric thresholds or rule-set here either — see [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE]. These are descriptive axes the end-user weighs for their own specifics, not a prescriptive cutover point.
- status: proposed
- provenance: david-stated for the use-case x complexity basis, the no-hard-thresholds stance, and the state_manager founding use-case (David, 2026-09-12, per [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE]); architect-synthesis for the spectrum's prose, the declarative/standalone/plugin-end framing, and the agentic-frontier-shifts-both-ways analysis.
- part-of: [SPEC:CXC-INTEGRATION-MODELS]
- see-also: [DX-DERIVE-ONCE-REALIZE-PER-LEAF], [DX-STATE-ON-BUS], [DX-NARROW-WAIST], [DX-COLLAPSE-TRANSPORT], [CXC-IM-MODEL-STATE-MANAGER-CONFIG], [CXC-IM-APP-GUIDANCE-IS-DESCRIPTIVE]

